.TH SSOOSSHD.YAML 5 "2026-08-25" "ssoossh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
ssoosshd.yaml \- configuration file for the ssoosshd server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssoosshd.yaml
is a YAML file configuring
.BR ssoosshd (8):
the HTTP(S) listener, TLS, the database, OIDC authentication, optional LDAP
enrichment, administrative and auditor access, web UI branding, outbound
email, the message broker, and the certificate-issuance policy for each
certificate type ssoosshd signs.
.SH LOCATION
Built-in defaults are loaded first (compiled into the binary; not a file on
disk), then exactly one config file is merged on top. If
.BR "ssoosshd --config " \fIfile\fR
is given, that file is used. Otherwise the following are searched, in
order, and the
.B first one found
is used \(em unlike the client's
.BR ssoossh.yaml (5),
these are not all merged together, so only one file's settings actually
apply:
.IP 1. 4
.I ./ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 2. 4
.I ~/.config/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 3. 4
.I ~/.config/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 4. 4
.I /etc/ssoosshd.yaml
.IP 5. 4
.I /etc/ssoossh/ssoosshd.yaml
.SH OPTIONS
Keys are shown as their full dotted path, e.g.
.B http.tls.min_version
means:
.RS
.nf
http:
  tls:
    min_version: ...
.fi
.RE
Every key below is top-level (or nested only as shown) in the actual config
schema.
.B ldap
and
.B authentication
are siblings of
.BR http ,
not nested under it; nesting them there is silently ignored by the config
loader.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED OPTIONS
.SS Top level
.TP
.BI traces: " bool"
Enables OpenTelemetry tracing, adding the otelgin middleware to the router. Exporter endpoints come from the standard OTEL_* environment variables.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI metrics: " bool"
Enables OpenTelemetry metrics. Exporter endpoints come from the standard OTEL_* environment variables.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI production: " bool"
Selects gin's release mode rather than debug mode, and gates the development\-only escape hatches: notably http.rate_limit_disable_for_dev is honored only when this is false, so rate limiting cannot be switched off in production.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI ssh_key: " string"
The SSH CA private key used to sign issued certificates. Inline PEM, not a file path. Exactly one of ssh_key or hsm may be set, and one of them must be: startup fails without a CA key.
.IP
.nf
ssh_key: |
.fi
.nf
\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY\-\-\-\-\-
.fi
.nf
\-\-\-\-\-END OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY\-\-\-\-\-
.fi
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI max_cert_lifetime: " duration"
The maximum lifetime for user/service/PAM certificates, enforced as a defense\-in\-depth check before signing. Must be greater than zero; a non\-positive value fails startup.
Default:
.BR 2160h .
.TP
.BI max_service_cert_lifetime: " duration"
The maximum lifetime for service certificates, enforced as a defense\-in\-depth check before signing. Service enrollments default to 8760h (cert_options.service valid_duration), so this cap carries headroom over its default. Must be greater than zero; a non\-positive value fails startup.
Default:
.BR 17544h .
.TP
.BI fips: " bool"
Steers the server toward FIPS 140\-3 approved algorithms: the CA key (checked at startup), client\-submitted public keys (checked in when a request is approved and again by the signer), and the TLS cipher/curve profile. A non\-approved algorithm is a hard error when this is in effect.
.IP
Unset is distinguishable from an explicit false: unset follows the Go runtime's own FIPS 140\-3 mode, crypto/fips140.Enabled(), which is why this key ships with no value rather than set to false.
.IP
.nf
fips: true
.fi
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI multi_instance: " bool"
Declares that the server is part of a multi\-instance deployment (multiple ssoosshd processes sharing a database). When enabled, certain checks are enforced (e.g., cookie_key must be explicitly set) and behaviors adapt to account for cross\-instance message delivery (a client waiting on one instance is woken by another) payloads). See docs/dev/multi\-instance\-safety\-plan.md.
Default:
.BR false .
.SS logging (the main application log)
.TP
.B logging.*
Log\-file rotation, via the embedded timberjack logger: filename, maxsize, maxage, maxbackups, localtime, compression, rotationinterval, backuptimeformat, rotateatminutes, rotateat, appendtimeafterext, and filemode.
.IP
An unset filename means no log file is written and output goes to stdout instead. maxsize is in megabytes (default 100); maxage is in days (default: keep forever); maxbackups caps retained rotated files (default: keep all, subject to maxage); localtime uses local time rather than UTC in rotated filenames; compression is none, gzip, or zstd; rotationinterval is a duration forcing rotation on a schedule in addition to size. The rest tune backup filename formatting and rotation at specific times. See the DeRuina/timberjack package for the per\-field detail.
.TP
.BI logging.level: " string"
The minimum log level: a level name (debug, info, warn, error, case\-insensitive, optionally with a numeric +N or \-N offset) or a raw numeric slog level such as \-4, 0, 4, or 8. An unrecognized or empty value falls back to info.
Default:
.BR WARN .
.TP
.BI logging.enable_stdout: " bool"
Also writes the main log to stdout even when a filename is set. When no filename is set, or the process is attached to a terminal, stdout is used regardless.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI logging.include_app_name: " bool"
Adds an "app" attribute, the string "ssoossh", to every log record.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI logging.include_app_version: " bool"
Adds a "version" attribute to every log record.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI logging.log_json: " bool"
Writes JSON instead of text. Text is meant to be read, not parsed: the message is written as bare prose so quoted values in it are not escaped, which is not a machine\-readable format. Set this wherever a log collector has to pull fields back out.
Default:
.BR false .
.SS db (the database connection, pooling, retries, and query logging)
.TP
.BI db.provider: " string"
Identifies which database backend to use.
Default:
.BR sqlite .
.TP
.BI db.connection_string: " string"
The connection string / DSN. For SQLite this is a file path (or ":memory:" for in\-memory); for PostgreSQL a standard postgres:// URL or keyword string.
Default:
.BR ssoossh.db .
.TP
.B db.logging.*
Log\-file rotation for this destination, via the embedded timberjack logger, with the same keys and meanings as logging.* above. This destination is only split out of the main log once its filename is set; until then its records go to the general log.
.TP
.BI db.logging.level: " string"
The minimum log level for this destination, in the same form as logging.level.
Default:
.BR WARN .
.TP
.BI db.logging.add_source: " bool"
Includes the source file and line on each record.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI db.logging.log_json: " bool"
Writes JSON instead of text. Text is meant to be read, not parsed: the message is written as bare prose so quoted values in it are not escaped, which is not a machine\-readable format. Set this wherever a log collector has to pull fields back out.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI db.retry_attempts: " int"
The maximum number of connection attempts before giving up. Zero or negative disables retries (will fail immediately on the first error). Applies to both SQLite (typically succeeds immediately) and PostgreSQL (may retry on transient network failures).
Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.BI db.retry_interval: " duration"
The time to wait between connection retry attempts. Only used if retry_attempts > 1. Applies to both SQLite and PostgreSQL.
Default:
.BR 3s .
.TP
.BI db.max_open_conns: " int"
Sets the maximum number of open connections to the database. Zero means Go's default (typically 2). For file\-backed SQLite, keep this modest: writes serialize on the write lock regardless, so high counts just queue without benefit. For PostgreSQL, scale with expected concurrency. This is ignored for in\-memory SQLite, which is always forced to 1.
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BI db.max_idle_conns: " int"
Sets the maximum number of idle connections held open. Zero means Go's default (typically 2, same as max_open_conns). Keep this proportionate to max_open_conns \(em they are not independent; a high max_open_conns with a low max_idle_conns causes expensive churn.
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BI db.conn_max_lifetime: " duration"
Sets the maximum amount of time a connection can be reused. Zero disables the limit (connections are reused indefinitely). Useful for breaking stale connections in long\-running deployments, but typically not necessary with a well\-behaved database server.
Default:
.BR 0 .
.SS http (the listener, TLS, sessions, and rate limiting)
.TP
.B http.access_logging.*
Log\-file rotation for the access log, via the embedded timberjack logger, with the same keys and meanings as logging.* above. The access log is only split into its own file once its filename is set.
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.level: " string"
The minimum log level for the access log, in the same form as logging.level. Requests are logged at INFO, client errors (4xx) at WARN and server errors (5xx) at ERROR, so "info" logs every request and "warn" logs only the failures.
.IP
This is what lets the access log be more verbose than the application log: the two are filtered independently, and both still reach stdout. Left empty the access log has no threshold of its own and is filtered at logging.level along with everything else \(em which, with the shipped logging.level of WARN, means no successful request is ever logged.
Default:
.BR info .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_user_agent: " bool"
Records the request's User\-Agent header.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_request_header: " bool"
Records the full request header set. Verbose, and it captures whatever the client sent.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_client_ip: " bool"
Records the client address, as resolved through http.trusted_proxies.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_request_id: " bool"
Records the per\-request correlation ID.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_request_body: " bool"
Records the request body. Off by default and worth leaving off: request bodies on this server carry public keys and enrollment codes.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_response_body: " bool"
Records the response body. Off by default and worth leaving off: response bodies carry issued certificates and enrollment tokens.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_response_header: " bool"
Records the full response header set.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_span_id: " bool"
Records the OpenTelemetry span ID.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_trace_id: " bool"
Records the OpenTelemetry trace ID.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.access_logging.log_json: " bool"
Writes JSON instead of text. Text is meant to be read, not parsed: the message is written as bare prose so quoted values in it are not escaped, which is not a machine\-readable format. Set this wherever a log collector has to pull fields back out.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.address: " string"
The network interface to bind to, e.g. "127.0.0.1" to listen only locally, or "0.0.0.0" for all interfaces. Used with Port to form the server's listen address.
Default:
.BR 127.0.0.1 .
.TP
.BI http.port: " int"
The TCP port to listen on, e.g. 80 for HTTP or 443 for HTTPS. Port 0 is reserved for testing and tells the OS to pick an unused port; the actual port is logged on startup. Ignored when unix_socket is set.
Default:
.BR 8080 .
.TP
.BI http.unix_socket: " string"
when set, is a filesystem path to listen on a Unix domain socket instead of TCP \(em Address and Port are ignored. Mutually exclusive with proxy_protocol (PROXY protocol is a TCP\-connection concept; it has nothing to prefix on a Unix socket).
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.proxy_protocol: " list"
Lists the CIDR ranges of reverse proxies trusted to prefix connections with a PROXY protocol v1/v2 header (so the real client address survives a TCP\-level proxy, as opposed to trusted_proxies below which is for HTTP\-level proxies setting X\-Forwarded\-For). Empty disables PROXY protocol support entirely; connections from any other source are rejected outright once this is set. Ignored (must be empty) when unix_socket is set.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.trusted_proxies: " list"
Lists the CIDR ranges of reverse proxies trusted to set X\-Forwarded\-For/X\-Forwarded\-Proto, passed to gin's SetTrustedProxies. Empty means no proxy is trusted, so c.ClientIP() always reports the direct connection's address and a client\-supplied X\-Forwarded\-For is ignored.
.IP
That only holds because the router passes this to SetTrustedProxies unconditionally \(em gin's own default is to trust every proxy, so skipping the call for an empty list would mean the opposite.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.server_name: " string"
when set, is the host name this server answers to: requests addressed to anything else (by Host header, or SNI on TLS connections) are rejected with 421 Misdirected Request by middleware.ServerNameMiddleware. The health endpoints are registered ahead of the check so probes can reach the server by IP. Empty disables the check. It plays no role in the TLS handshake itself, which is why it does not live in TLSConfig.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.public_url: " string"
The scheme and host browsers actually reach this deployment at, e.g. "https://ssh.example.com". Set it whenever that differs from what Address/Port/TLS describe \(em which is every reverse\-proxy deployment, since the proxy terminates TLS on 443 while this process listens on plain HTTP somewhere else.
.IP
Two things are derived from it and cannot be got right without it: the OIDC redirect URI handed to the identity provider, and the origin the CSRF middleware compares the browser's Origin header against. Both used to be reconstructed from server_name plus the *listen* port, which is only the public port when nothing sits in front.
.IP
Its scheme also settles the derived HTTPS flag, so a deployment behind a TLS\-terminating proxy needs this or is_https, not both.
.IP
Origin only: a path, query, or fragment is rejected at startup. Serving the app under a sub\-path would need the frontend's base to move with it, which is not supported, so accepting one here would only produce a redirect URI that silently does not work.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.is_https: " bool"
Records that a reverse proxy terminates TLS while this process serves plain HTTP, so the deployment is HTTPS as browsers see it even though this listener is not. Redundant when public_url is set \(em its scheme settles the same question \(em so configure one or the other, not both.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_key: " string"
The secret used to sign and encrypt session cookies. If empty, a key is generated once and persisted in the server_secrets table, so sessions survive a restart and instances sharing a database share the key. Configure an explicit value to key it from outside the database.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_secure: " bool"
Marks the session cookie Secure, so browsers only send it over HTTPS. Unset derives it from whether the deployment is HTTPS at all (see is_https), which keeps plain\-HTTP local development working while defaulting to on everywhere else. Set it explicitly only to override that inference.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_same_site: " string"
Controls the session cookie's SameSite attribute: "strict" (default), "lax", or "none". Strict is right for this server because nothing legitimately navigates into it from another site \(em the OIDC callback is a redirect the browser follows to a URL this server issued, not a cross\-site form post.
.IP
This is defence in depth, not the CSRF control: SameSite does nothing against an attacker page on a different origin of the *same* site, so state\-changing routes are additionally guarded by the CSRF middleware.
Default:
.BR strict .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_max_age: " duration"
The absolute ceiling on a session's lifetime, measured from login. Activity does not extend it: once a session is this old the next request is unauthenticated regardless of how recently it was used, and the user signs in again. Zero uses the built\-in default.
Default:
.BR 9h .
.TP
.BI http.cookie_idle_timeout: " duration"
How long a session survives without a request. SessionAuthMiddleware slides this window on activity (re\-saving the session past half the window, which reissues the cookie), so an actively\-used session only ever ends at cookie_max_age, while an abandoned browser expires after this much quiet. Zero uses the built\-in default. Must not exceed cookie_max_age \- an idle window longer than the absolute cap cannot ever be reached.
Default:
.BR 30m .
.TP
.BI http.rate_limit: " int"
The maximum number of requests per rate_duration allowed per client IP. Zero or negative disables rate limiting entirely. Localhost (127.0.0.1, ::1) bypasses the limit, so health probes and tests from the local frontend are not throttled.
Default:
.BR 60 .
.TP
.BI http.rate_duration: " duration"
The time window for rate limiting calculations. With rate_limit=60 and rate_duration=1m, each IP is allowed 60 requests per minute. Typical values are in seconds to minutes.
Default:
.BR 1m .
.TP
.BI http.rate_limit_disable_for_dev: " bool"
Disables all rate limiting (global and per\-endpoint) when true. Only effective when Production=false, so it cannot be silently enabled in production. Used for local development to avoid throttling during testing.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI http.cert_request_rate_limit.user: " number"
The limit for POST /api/certs/user (interactive user cert).
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BI http.cert_request_rate_limit.service_enroll: " number"
The limit for POST /api/certs/service/enroll (service enrollment, unattended).
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BI http.cert_request_rate_limit.pam: " number"
The limit for POST /api/certs/pam (short\-lived PAM cert, gated on local interaction).
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BI http.service_code_rate_limit.limit: " number"
Requests per second per enrollment code. Zero or negative disables this limit.
Default:
.BR 1 .
.TP
.BI http.hsts: " string"
(HTTP Strict\-Transport\-Security) is sent in the Strict\-Transport\-Security header on every response, but only when the server terminates TLS itself (i.e. tls.certificate_file and tls.private_key_file are set). Browsers ignore HSTS over plain HTTP. Empty disables the header, useful when a TLS\-terminating reverse proxy in front sets its own policy. Typical values include "max\-age=31536000; includeSubDomains".
Default:
.BR max\-age=31536000; includeSubDomains .
.TP
.BI http.tls.cipher_suites: " list"
Restricts the TLS 1.0\-1.2 cipher suites offered (Go does not allow configuring TLS 1.3 suites). Names must exactly match crypto/tls constant names, case\-sensitively, e.g. "TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256"; the stdlib's insecure suites resolve too. Empty means Go's defaults. Any explicit list must include TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 or TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256: net/http's automatic HTTP/2 setup refuses to serve without one of them, failing the server at startup.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.tls.min_version: " string"
The minimum TLS protocol version: TLS1.0, TLS1.1, TLS1.2, or TLS1.3. Parsing tolerates case, "."/"\-"/"_"/" " separators, and the "tls"/"version" prefixes ("tls12", "TLS\-1.2", and "VersionTLS12" all work). TLS1.0 and TLS1.1 resolve but log a deprecation warning (RFC 8996).
Default:
.BR TLS1.3 .
.TP
.BI http.tls.curves: " list"
Restricts the key\-exchange curves/groups offered, in preference order: P256, P384, P521, X25519, or X25519MLKEM768, with the same tolerant parsing as TLSMinVersion ("CurveP256", "p\-256", ...). Empty means Go's defaults; note that naming any curve replaces those defaults entirely rather than adding to them.
Default:
.BR empty .
.TP
.BI http.tls.reload_interval: " duration"
How often the certificate files are re\-read on a timer, in addition to SIGHUP which always reloads. Zero or negative disables the timer, leaving SIGHUP as the only trigger.
.IP
The timer exists for deployments where nothing signals the process: a Kubernetes secret remount is the motivating case, since it replaces a directory symlink rather than writing the files in place, so a filesystem watch on the paths never fires.
Default:
.BR 0 .
